@@ -0,0 +1,16 @@
+# CS Web
+
+CS Web is an unofficial desktop browser FPS. One human and nine bots play a 5v5 match. The game ID is `cs-web`. Its static entry document lives at `/cs-web/`, and the game’s own Vite development server uses port 5177.
+
+The catalog card is enabled when `VITE_ENABLE_CS_WEB=true` at hub build time. For production, enable the corresponding Actions variable after a reviewed game build is served on the hub’s origin.
+
+## Run both sites locally
+
+1. Install the dependencies in the CS Web and Game Hub checkouts with `npm ci`.
+2. Configure both checkouts’ ignored `.env.local` files with the same local Supabase URL and publishable key. Start local Supabase as described in the hub backend guide. The hub’s catalog requires guest entry. The standalone game runs without a configured backend.
+3. From the game checkout run `npm test` and `npm run build`.
+4. Set `VITE_ENABLE_CS_WEB=true` in the hub’s `.env.local` and run `npm run build`. Set `CS_WEB_SITE_DIR` to the absolute game checkout path when it is outside the default sibling location.
+5. From the hub checkout run `node --env-file=.env.local scripts/serve-session-test.mjs`. Visit `http://127.0.0.1:4175/game-hub/`, enter a guest name, and select **Play CS Web**. Use **Back to Game Hub** to return.
+6. Stop the server before running `npm run test:cs-web`. Playwright starts the same-origin server for the test. Install the Chromium test browser once with `npx playwright install chromium`.
+
+The standalone client restores verified guest display names and uses locally saved names when guest identity is unavailable. Shared rooms and network play are outside this release. The game’s security policy allows WebAssembly for its bundled Rapier physics engine.
